---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { estimateCatHumanAge, estimateDogHumanAge } from '../../../utils/agingModel';
const breadcrumbs = [{ label: 'Home', url: '/en/' }, { label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' }, { label: 'Dog Years vs Cat Years', url: '/en/blog/dog-years-vs-cat-years/' }];
const siteUrl = 'https://www.petaginghub.com';
const hrefLangAlternates = [
  { lang: 'en', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/dog-years-vs-cat-years' },
  { lang: 'x-default', url: siteUrl + '/en/blog/dog-years-vs-cat-years' },
];
const comparisonAges = [1, 2, 5, 10, 15];
---
<BaseLayout title="Dog Years vs Cat Years: Are They the Same? | Pet Aging Hub" description="Are cat years the same as dog years? Compare educational age estimates, see why dog size matters, and learn what these numbers cannot tell you about health." lang="en" breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} hrefLangAlternates={hrefLangAlternates}>
  <article class="w-[calc(100vw-4rem)] max-w-3xl mx-auto py-10 px-4 space-y-8">
    <header>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">Dog Years vs Cat Years: Are They the Same?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">No. Dogs and cats have different life-stage patterns, and dog size also matters. A single “one pet year equals seven human years” rule cannot represent either species accurately. Human-age equivalents on this site are educational comparisons, not a measure of an individual animal's health.</p>
    </header>
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Do cats age like dogs?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">Both species mature quickly early in life, but their later-life estimates differ. Our cat estimate starts at about 15 human-equivalent years at one year and 24 at two years, then adds about four per year. Our dog estimate also accounts for adult size; it is a site-derived illustration, not an AAHA or AVMA conversion formula.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">Multiplying calendar age by seven misses that early growth: a two-year-old cat is about 24 in this educational comparison, not 14. It also hides the difference between a small and a giant dog of the same calendar age. The table below shows those assumptions openly so you can compare them without treating either result as a diagnosis.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Compare the site's estimates</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 mb-4">The numbers below come from the same shared model as our calculators. They are not veterinary life-stage classifications and should not be used to diagnose aging or disease.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="bg-slate-800 text-white"><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Calendar age</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Cat</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Small dog</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Medium dog</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Large dog</th><th scope="col" class="p-3 text-left">Giant dog</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{comparisonAges.map((age) => <tr class="border-b border-slate-200"><th scope="row" class="p-3 text-left">{age} years</th><td class="p-3">{estimateCatHumanAge(age)}</td><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(age, 'small')}</td><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(age, 'medium')}</td><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(age, 'large')}</td><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(age, 'giant')}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mt-3">Pet Aging Hub uses its own practical dog-size bands and approximate conversion. Individual breeds and animals vary. For a personalized input, use the <a href="/en/tools/compare-aging/" class="text-blue-700 underline">interactive comparison tool</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Why does size matter for dogs?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">Dogs vary much more in adult size than domestic cats. Veterinary senior-care guidance therefore does not give every dog one fixed senior birthday: it defines the senior period relative to estimated lifespan. Cats are classified separately: <a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2023-aaha-senior-care-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/defining-the-senior-patient/" class="text-blue-700 underline">AAHA senior-care guidelines</a> describe dogs as senior during roughly the last quarter of estimated lifespan and cats as senior after age 10. These life stages are separate from the human-age numbers above.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">What can't a human-age estimate tell you?</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">It cannot determine biological age, predict an individual lifespan, or explain changes in appetite, weight, movement, or behavior. Discuss meaningful changes with a veterinarian rather than attributing them to a number. For broader species and care differences, read <a href="/en/blog/dog-vs-cat-aging/" class="text-blue-700 underline">how dogs and cats age differently</a>; for health monitoring, see the <a href="/en/blog/senior-pet-health-guide/" class="text-blue-700 underline">senior pet health guide</a>.</p>
      <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">If you are checking one animal rather than comparing species, use the <a href="/en/tools/dog-age-calculator/" class="text-blue-700 underline">dog age calculator</a> or <a href="/en/tools/cat-age-calculator/" class="text-blue-700 underline">cat age calculator</a>. Changes you observe are better addressed in the <a href="/en/blog/dog-aging-signs/" class="text-blue-700 underline">dog aging signs</a> and <a href="/en/blog/cat-aging-signs/" class="text-blue-700 underline">cat aging signs</a> guides; age alone cannot tell you whether a symptom is expected.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="text-sm text-slate-600 border-t pt-5">
      <h2 class="font-semibold text-slate-800 mb-2">Source and method</h2>
      <p>Veterinary life-stage context: <a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2023-aaha-senior-care-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/defining-the-senior-patient/" class="text-blue-700 underline">AAHA, Defining the Senior Patient</a>. Human-age table: Pet Aging Hub's educational model, not a veterinary guideline.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
